# The three-star question: how Alice herself changes across the book, from the girl who cries in the
# hall to the one who stands up to the court, with a quotation from each end.
item reading.alice-changed v=1 stars=3 skills=[reading.characters, reading.evidence, reading.whole-book, writing.composition] {
  title "How has Alice changed?"

  scene 34x27 {
    column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
      row top space=2 align=end {
        bookcast a who=alice pose=hold dir=1
        text ask "Choose a moment from the first chapters and a moment from the last. Copy a few words from each, and say how Alice has changed and what changed her." width=24
      }
      writinglines lines lines=6 width=33 prompt=""
    }
  }

  check writing.by-eye look-for="An early moment (crying in the hall in chapter 2, doing as the Rabbit orders in chapter 4, being snubbed by the Caterpillar) and a late one (arguing with the King, refusing to leave the court, calling them a pack of cards in chapter 12), a few words copied exactly from each, and a reason for the change (she has learnt that the creatures' rules make no sense, or she grows used to standing up for herself)." notice=["Picks an early and a late moment", "Copies words exactly", "Says how she changed", "Gives a reason for the change"] ask="Which creature would you have stood up to first?"
  hint "How does Alice answer when someone orders her about in chapter 4? And in chapter 12?"
  hint "What has she learnt about these creatures by the end?"
}
